early independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139534732 true] [17592186124330 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186124330 1100 "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$." 13194139534732 false] [17592186124330 1145 "From the diagonal matrix $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186124330 1145 "From the diagonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1= -{2}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2\n$$" 13194139534732 false] [17592186124331 1100 "Given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP$." 13194139534732 false] [17592186124331 1100 "Given the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186124331 1145 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-\\frac{5}{8})R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2$, we have\n$A - (-2)I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534732 false] [17592186124331 1145 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186124332 1100 "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." 13194139534732 true] [17592186124332 1100 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." 13194139534732 false] [17592186124332 1145 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nwe construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P$, we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns:\n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$, and\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534732 false] [17592186124332 1145 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186124520 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306212 13194139534732 true] [17592186124520 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306