ch that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." 13194139534732 true] [17592186125003 1145 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186125004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123046 13194139534732 false] [17592186125004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306274 13194139534732 true] [17592186125004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534732 true] [17592186125004 1100 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186125004 1100 "If the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139534732 false] [17592186125004 1145 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139534732 true] [17592186125006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123058 13194139534732 false] [17592186125006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306280 13194139534732 true] [17592186125006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534732 true] [17592186125006 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" 13194139534732 true] [17592186125006 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$ and $λ_{2} = 15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$?" 13194139534732 false] [17592186125006 1145 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is